WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.525
The repository is the only
normal path to production.

2
00:00:03.525 --> 00:00:08.803
Six change-control rules, which together
are how the program meets CM-3 and

3
00:00:08.803 --> 00:00:14.513
CM-5 for the identity plane and FedRAMP
20x's change-management indicators.

4
00:00:14.513 --> 00:00:18.963
One, two people per change: the author
of a pull request can't approve it,

5
00:00:18.963 --> 00:00:23.570
and Tier 0 objects, such as emergency
exclusions and Tier 0 PIM settings,

6
00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.537
need a second approver
from the security team.

7
00:00:26.537 --> 00:00:31.656
Two, checks before merge: schema
validation, the emergency exclusion check,

8
00:00:31.656 --> 00:00:36.113
the safety rules, and a plan or dry
run attached to the pull request.

9
00:00:36.113 --> 00:00:40.977
Three, report-only first; a policy runs
at least a week against the sign-in

10
00:00:40.977 --> 00:00:45.924
logs and is enabled in a second pull
request that cites the impact review.

11
00:00:45.924 --> 00:00:49.117
Four, the pipeline signs
in as a service principal

12
00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:52.075
with workload identity
federation, no secret.

13
00:00:52.075 --> 00:00:56.338
It can change every access policy in
the tenant, so it's a Tier 0 asset,

14
00:00:56.338 --> 00:00:59.672
with two owners, quarterly
permission reviews and a

15
00:00:59.672 --> 00:01:03.625
workload identity policy
restricting where it signs in from.

16
00:01:03.625 --> 00:01:08.546
Five, emergency changes in the portal happen
under a PIM activation with a ticket,

17
00:01:08.546 --> 00:01:11.190
and are back-ported
within one business day;

18
00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.863
until then the drift monitor
reports them, which is the point.

19
00:01:14.863 --> 00:01:19.649
Six, rollback is a revert, so it
has the same record as the change.

20
00:01:19.649 --> 00:01:22.196
Drift detection catches
what code can't stop,

21
00:01:22.196 --> 00:01:26.187
because a Global Administrator can
still change the tenant directly.

22
00:01:26.187 --> 00:01:29.163
The tenant monitor's limits
shape how it's used.

23
00:01:29.163 --> 00:01:31.862
It runs every six hours, fixed.

24
00:01:31.862 --> 00:01:36.937
Snapshots expire after seven days, resolved
drifts are deleted thirty days after

25
00:01:36.937 --> 00:01:41.937
they're resolved, and updating a baseline
deletes that monitor's earlier results.

26
00:01:41.937 --> 00:01:44.356
Those are evidence rules in disguise:

27
00:01:44.356 --> 00:01:47.475
export every result before
changing a baseline.

28
00:01:47.475 --> 00:01:49.750
Every drift is one of two things.

29
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:51.693
Unauthorized or accidental:

30
00:01:51.693 --> 00:01:56.388
redeploy the repository's version
and investigate from the audit log.

31
00:01:56.388 --> 00:02:00.601
Intended but not yet in code:
back-port it by pull request.

32
00:02:00.601 --> 00:02:04.948
A drift open more than one business
day goes to the governance office,

33
00:02:04.948 --> 00:02:08.140
and drift on a Tier 0 object
is a security incident.
